description:
# Phase 4 — OpenAI Codex PKCE oauth flow + token storage + refresh

## context

Read the parent epic first. Phase 3 registered the `openai_codex_responses` provider but fails cleanly when `AuthStorage` has no codex credentials. Phase 4 delivers the full PKCE oauth flow so users can `zi login openai-codex`, get redirected to ChatGPT, consent, land back on a local loopback, and have their access+refresh tokens persisted in `auth.json` under the `openai-codex` key.

This is the juiciest phase because it involves:
- PKCE code challenge generation (SHA256 + base64url)
- Local HTTP loopback server (one-shot, ephemeral port)
- Browser launch
- Token exchange
- Refresh-token rotation on 401 during streaming
- Secure storage

## scope

~600 lines of new zig across 3 new files + modifications to `src/auth/storage.zig` and the codex provider from phase 3.

## pi-mono reference (READ BEFORE WRITING)

Trace these files carefully — the WHAT must match byte-for-byte on the oauth wire:

- `.references/pi-mono/packages/coding-agent/src/auth/openai-codex-login.ts` (or similar; grep for `chatgpt.com/backend-api/oauth` or `PKCE`)
- `.references/pi-mono/packages/ai/src/providers/openai-codex.ts` — how the provider attaches the bearer token, handles 401, triggers refresh
- `.references/pi-mono/packages/coding-agent/src/auth/storage.ts` — how the token record is shaped in auth.json

Find them with:

```bash
rg -l "PKCE|code_challenge|code_verifier|chatgpt.com/backend-api/oauth" .references/pi-mono/
rg -l "openai-codex" .references/pi-mono/packages/
```

## PKCE flow (standard, from RFC 7636)

```
1. generate code_verifier = base64url(random 32 bytes)
2. code_challenge = base64url(sha256(code_verifier))
3. open browser:
     https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/login/chatgpt/auth
       ?client_id=<pi-mono's registered client id — trace from pi-mono>
       &redirect_uri=http://localhost:<ephemeral_port>/callback
       &response_type=code
       &code_challenge=<challenge>
       &code_challenge_method=S256
       &scope=<scopes — trace from pi-mono>
       &state=<random>
4. loopback server accepts GET /callback?code=<code>&state=<state>
5. verify state matches
6. POST https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/login/chatgpt/token
     grant_type=authorization_code
     code=<code>
     code_verifier=<verifier>
     redirect_uri=<same as step 3>
     client_id=<same>
   → { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, ... }
7. persist { access_token, refresh_token, expires_at } to auth.json["openai-codex"]
8. respond to the browser with a success HTML page and shut down the loopback
```

The client_id and exact endpoint paths MUST come from pi-mono — do not guess. If pi-mono uses a different scheme (e.g. device flow, or a different redirect_uri), match it.

## new files

### `src/auth/pkce.zig` (~80 lines)

Pure crypto helpers. No I/O.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const crypto = std.crypto;

pub const Pkce = struct {
    verifier: [43]u8,  // base64url of 32 random bytes = 43 chars unpadded
    challenge: [43]u8, // base64url of sha256 of verifier

    pub fn generate() Pkce {
        var raw: [32]u8 = undefined;
        crypto.random.bytes(&raw);
        var verifier: [43]u8 = undefined;
        _ = std.base64.url_safe_no_pad.Encoder.encode(&verifier, &raw);

        var hash: [32]u8 = undefined;
        crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256.hash(&verifier, &hash, .{});
        var challenge: [43]u8 = undefined;
        _ = std.base64.url_safe_no_pad.Encoder.encode(&challenge, &hash);

        return .{ .verifier = verifier, .challenge = challenge };
    }
};

pub fn randomState(allocator: std.mem.Allocator) ![]u8 {
    var raw: [16]u8 = undefined;
    crypto.random.bytes(&raw);
    const encoded = try allocator.alloc(u8, 22);  // base64url of 16 bytes
    _ = std.base64.url_safe_no_pad.Encoder.encode(encoded, &raw);
    return encoded;
}
```

Tests: 2 — (1) verifier/challenge shape assertions, (2) SHA256 vector against a known RFC 7636 test case.

### `src/auth/loopback.zig` (~200 lines)

One-shot HTTP server on an ephemeral port. Accepts exactly ONE `GET /callback?code=...&state=...`, validates the state, responds with success HTML, closes. Fatal if anything else.

Uses `std.net.StreamServer` (or whatever zig 0.15.2 calls it — check the zig skill). Bind to `127.0.0.1:0` to get an ephemeral port, read back via `getsockname`-equivalent.

Key structure:

```zig
pub const LoopbackResult = struct {
    code: []const u8,
    state: []const u8,
};

pub const Loopback = struct {
    server: std.net.Server,
    port: u16,
    allocator: std.mem.Allocator,

    pub fn start(allocator: std.mem.Allocator) !Loopback { ... }
    pub fn port(self: *const Loopback) u16 { return self.port; }

    /// Blocks until exactly one callback arrives, or timeout_ms elapses.
    /// Validates that the path is /callback and query params include code+state.
    /// Writes a fixed success HTML to the socket before closing.
    /// Caller owns the returned strings.
    pub fn awaitCallback(
        self: *Loopback,
        allocator: std.mem.Allocator,
        timeout_ms: u64,
    ) !LoopbackResult { ... }

    pub fn deinit(self: *Loopback) void { ... }
};
```

Be strict on the request parser — this is a narrow one-shot path, not a general HTTP server. Only accept `GET /callback?...` with a well-formed query string. Reject anything else with 400 + close.

Threading: this runs on a short-lived **login worker thread** per the AGENTS.md threading doctrine (lines 65-72 — "login worker thread" is already sanctioned as a short-lived helper). Do NOT block the TUI or agent thread.

Security: bind to 127.0.0.1 only, never 0.0.0.0. Validate `Host` header. Validate state matches.

### `src/auth/openai_codex_login.zig` (~250 lines)

The orchestration layer. Public API:

```zig
pub const CodexTokens = struct {
    access_token: []const u8,
    refresh_token: []const u8,
    expires_at_ms: i64,  // unix ms
};

/// Run the full PKCE login flow:
///   1. generate PKCE + state
///   2. start loopback on ephemeral port
///   3. open browser to ChatGPT auth URL
///   4. await callback
///   5. exchange code for tokens
///   6. return tokens (caller persists via AuthStorage.setCodexTokens)
pub fn login(
    allocator: std.mem.Allocator,
    cancel: abort_signal.AbortSignal,
) !CodexTokens;

/// Exchange a refresh_token for a new access_token.
/// Called by the provider on 401 during streaming.
pub fn refresh(
    allocator: std.mem.Allocator,
    refresh_token: []const u8,
) !CodexTokens;
```

Browser-opening: use `open` on macOS, `xdg-open` on linux, `start` on windows. Use `std.process.Child`, don't block on exit.

Token exchange: POST with `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body to pi-mono's exact token endpoint. Parse JSON response via `std.json.parseFromSlice`.

## modifications

### `src/auth/storage.zig`

Add typed codex accessors:

```zig
pub fn getCodexTokens(self: *AuthStorage) ?CodexTokens {
    self.mu.lock();
    defer self.mu.unlock();
    // read auth.json["openai-codex"] as a structured record, not a flat api key
    ...
}

pub fn setCodexTokens(self: *AuthStorage, tokens: CodexTokens) !void {
    self.mu.lock();
    defer self.mu.unlock();
    // persist to auth.json["openai-codex"]
    ...
}

pub fn clearCodexTokens(self: *AuthStorage) !void { ... }
```

Update `hasAuth("openai-codex")` to check that `access_token` exists AND `expires_at_ms > now` OR `refresh_token` exists.

### `src/ai/openai_codex.zig` (from phase 3)

Wire real auth:

```zig
fn getAccessToken(self: *OpenAICodexProvider) ![]const u8 {
    const tokens = self.auth_storage.getCodexTokens() orelse return error.NotLoggedIn;

    const now = std.time.milliTimestamp();
    if (tokens.expires_at_ms > now + 30_000) {
        return tokens.access_token;
    }

    // Refresh
    const fresh = try openai_codex_login.refresh(self.allocator, tokens.refresh_token);
    try self.auth_storage.setCodexTokens(fresh);
    return fresh.access_token;
}
```

On 401 during streaming, retry ONCE with a fresh token from `refresh()`. If the refresh itself 401s, clear stored tokens and return `error.NotLoggedIn` to force re-login.

### `src/main.zig` or `src/login/` — add `zi login openai-codex` subcommand

Check existing login path (grep for `login` in main.zig). The subcommand:

```
zi login openai-codex
```

Runs on the login worker thread:
1. Prints "opening browser..."
2. Calls `openai_codex_login.login(...)`
3. On success: `auth_storage.setCodexTokens(...)`, prints "Logged in as <account>"
4. On failure: prints error, exit 1

## verification

```bash
zig build

# manual test
unset OPENAI_API_KEY
./zig-out/bin/zi login openai-codex
# browser opens, consent, lands on success page
./zig-out/bin/zi -p --model "openai-codex/gpt-5.4" "say hi"
# streams successfully

# refresh test — mangle auth.json to set expires_at_ms to past
jq '."openai-codex".expires_at_ms = 0' ~/.zi/agent/auth.json > /tmp/a && mv /tmp/a ~/.zi/agent/auth.json
./zig-out/bin/zi -p --model "openai-codex/gpt-5.4" "say hi"
# should refresh transparently, still stream

# no-auth test
./zig-out/bin/zi logout openai-codex
./zig-out/bin/zi -p --model "openai-codex/gpt-5.4" "say hi"
# clean error, exit non-zero
```

## acceptance criteria

- [ ] `pkce.zig` generates verifier/challenge matching RFC 7636 test vectors
- [ ] `loopback.zig` binds to 127.0.0.1:0, accepts one callback, rejects everything else, times out cleanly
- [ ] `openai_codex_login.zig` implements full login + refresh against pi-mono's exact endpoints + client_id + scopes
- [ ] Tokens persist to `auth.json["openai-codex"]` with structured shape (access/refresh/expires_at)
- [ ] `AuthStorage.hasAuth("openai-codex")` returns true when tokens are present and either non-expired or refreshable
- [ ] Codex provider auto-refreshes on 401, retries once, clears on second 401
- [ ] `zi login openai-codex` subcommand works end-to-end
- [ ] `zi logout openai-codex` clears the stored tokens
- [ ] `zig build` clean, no goroutine-style hacks (real thread for loopback)
- [ ] Security: loopback is 127.0.0.1 only, Host header validated, state validated
- [ ] Tests: 2 pkce tests, 1 loopback smoke test (spawn thread, fire fake callback, assert parse)

## anti-patterns to refuse

- DO NOT bind to 0.0.0.0 or any non-loopback address.
- DO NOT skip state validation — CSRF risk.
- DO NOT hand-roll HTTP parsing beyond what the one-shot loopback needs.
- DO NOT guess pi-mono's client_id / scopes / endpoint paths. Trace them from `.references/pi-mono/`.
- DO NOT retry more than once on 401 — unbounded retry masks a broken refresh token.
- DO NOT log the access_token or refresh_token. Ever.
- DO NOT put the login flow on the agent or TUI thread. Use a dedicated login worker thread per AGENTS.md §Threading.
